# THE SIFREI ROWS on Numbers 28-29 (THE NUMBERS WALK sitting 9 — THE OFFERINGS CALENDAR, 2026-09-11): piskaot 142-152 by POSITION — the
# eleven between 141 (27:21) and 153 (30:2); TWENTY-THREE rows at the shelf's row grain, each opened and verdicted; the middah named where the
# row argues by one (logic/MIDDOT.md). NO piska on 29:1-11 or 29:13-34 (asserted in offerings_ink.py). The rows' own defects (a Hebrew
# proof-text garbled, a speaker dropped, Leviticus cited as Numbers, a name shortened, a word garbled) read to their verses (RESEARCH_LOG.md).
from offerings_ink import *
S = {}
def srow(p, r, verdict, prose): S[(p, r)] = (verdict, prose)

srow(142, 1, 'CONTEXT', '"COMMAND THE CHILDREN OF ISRAEL... MY OFFERING, MY BREAD" — WHY "COMMAND"? THE KING\'S DYING WIFE: CHARGE MY SONS OVER ME BEFORE YOU CHARGE ME OVER THEM. Because Moses had just asked for a shepherd "who will go out before them" (27:16-17), the LORD answers with the offerings: as a queen on her deathbed charged the king to care for her sons and the king replied "first charge my sons over me, that they not rebel and not cheapen me" — so "before you charge Me over My sons, charge them over Me, that they not exchange My honor for foreign gods" (Deuteronomy 31:19-20 the proof). The Hebrew row adds the standing rule of "command": "an urging at once, at the time of the act, and for the generations" (as at the head of Leviticus). The ink: 28:1-2 follow 27:23 (Joshua\'s appointment) — the parable reads the JUXTAPOSITION of the succession and the daily offering (E? the adjacency read, the aggadic register); the register: the portion\'s second speech-frame. Homiletic, the juxtaposition its ground; the "command" rule is law — CONTEXT for the calendar, the adjacency noted.')
srow(142, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"MY OFFERING" — THE BLOOD; "MY BREAD" — THE DEVOTED PORTIONS; "FOR MY FIRES" — THE FISTFULS AND THE FRANKINCENSE; "MY SWEET SAVOR" — THE LIBATIONS (THE HEBREW: THE SHOWBREAD\'S DISHES); "YOU SHALL KEEP" THRICE — FROM THE TEMPLE FUND, WITH THE THREE ORDERS STANDING BY, INSPECTED FOUR DAYS; "IN ITS APPOINTED TIME" — THE TAMID OVERRIDES THE SABBATH, AND THE IDENTITY WITH THE PESACH. The four possessives of 28:2 parsed into four things — the blood, the devoted portions (proved by "the bread of a fire-offering", Leviticus 3:16: "not the second formulation but the first"), the fistfuls with the frankincense, and "My sweet savor" — the ENGLISH says the libations, the HEBREW says THE SHOWBREAD\'S DISHES (בזיכי לחם הפנים — the frankincense dishes): the two rows differ on the fourth item; "you shall keep" read three times — the offering bought only from the chamber\'s fund; priests, Levites and Israelites STANDING over it (the ma\'amad); and an IDENTITY OF EXPRESSION (I2): "keep" here and "keep" of the Pesach lamb (Exodus 12:6) — inspected four days before slaughter. THEN THE SABBATH: "in its appointed time" — R. YOSHIYAH: the Pesach\'s "in its appointed time" (9:2) means even on the Sabbath; R. YONATAN objects "in this sense we have not heard it"; and R. YOSHIYAH ANSWERS (the Hebrew "said R. Yoshiyah to him" — THE ENGLISH DROPS THE SPEAKER and runs the answer into R. Yonatan\'s mouth, RESEARCH_LOG.md): the tamid\'s "in its appointed time" is not needed for the tamid\'s own Sabbath (28:9 already gives the Sabbath its lambs) — so it is FREED (מופנה) for the identity: "in its appointed time" here, "in its appointed time" there (9:2) — as the tamid overrides the Sabbath, so the Pesach. THE INK: "in its appointed time" three Torah seats — 9:2, 9:3 and 28:2 (computed; Hosea 2:11 the fourth) — the identity runs on exactly the Pesach\'s two and the tamid\'s one; the four possessives — "My offering", "for My fires", "My pleasing aroma" each ONE seat, "My bread" ten (computed). The middah: I2 (the identity of expression on a FREED term — the freeing argued from 28:9\'s redundancy). The exam: Pesachim 66a (Hillel\'s identity — "in its appointed time" — the Pesach overriding the Sabbath; the LINK REVIEW LAW\'s own exemplar), 77a; Yoma 46a; Menachot 49b-50a (the tamid\'s order); Shekalim 4:1 (the chamber\'s fund); Taanit 4:2 (the ma\'amadot); Arakhin 13a (the four days).')
srow(142, 3, 'MATERIAL', '"AND YOU SHALL SAY TO THEM" — AN EXHORTATION TO THE COURT; "TWO PER DAY" — SHIMON BEN AZZAI: OPPOSITE THE DAY, THE SUN — WHENCE THE MORNING LAMB AT THE NORTHWEST CORNER, THE EVENING LAMB AT THE NORTHEAST. "Say to them" — the court charged; "two per day" (28:3) — not "an obligation of the day" (28:4 already gives the morning and the evening), but OPPOSITE THE DAY: the slaughter placed by the sun — the morning tamid at the northwest corner on the second ring, the evening at the northeast (the Hebrew row: northeast for the morning, southwest for the evening — the two rows\' corners DIFFER; Tamid 4:1 and Yoma 62b the exam). The ink: "two per day" here and Exodus 29:38 (computed — the tamid\'s two seats); 28:4\'s "the one lamb... the second lamb" the day\'s obligation. The middah: the reading of a redundant phrase for a second content (E? — the word freed from its plain job by 28:4, ben Azzai\'s form). The exam: Mishnah Tamid 4:1, Yoma 62b (the rings and the corners).')
srow(142, 4, 'MATERIAL', '"THE ONE LAMB" — NOT MORE THAN ONE: TWO VERSES ("THIS IS THE FIRE-OFFERING", "THIS IS WHAT YOU SHALL OFFER", EXODUS 29:38) MIGHT HAVE MEANT FOUR. The two tamid commands (28:3 and Exodus 29:38) read together might double the count — "the one lamb" (28:4) closes it: neither add nor subtract (the Hebrew\'s "not to add and not to diminish"). The ink: 28:4 = Exodus 29:39 word for word but for THE ARTICLE — "the one lamb" (Exodus, האחד) against "the lamb, one" (Numbers, אחד bare; computed); the parser reads 1 and the ordinal "the second". The middah: E1 (the limitation "the one"). The exam: Menachot 4:4 (the temidim not indispensable to each other).')
srow(142, 5, 'MATERIAL', '"A TENTH OF AN EPHAH" — ONE OF TEN; "FLOUR" — OF WHEAT, FROM THE ONE PLACE THE TORAH SPECIFIES (EXODUS 29:2), ALL FLOURS ARE WHEAT; "BEATEN OIL" — NOT COOKED-OUT OIL, AND THE LAMP\'S THE SAME (LEVITICUS 24:2). "A tenth of the ephah" (28:5) — one in ten; "fine flour" — wheat, not barley, spelt, oats or rye: Exodus 29:2 says "wheat flour" once, and the specification at one place governs every unqualified "flour" in the Torah — the middah I3 (the generalization from one specified case — binyan av); "mixed with beaten oil" — excluding oil pressed by cooking, and the menorah\'s "clear beaten olive oil" (Leviticus 24:2) the same. The ink: "beaten oil" at Exodus 29:40 and here (the tamid\'s two seats; "beaten" five Bible tokens — Exodus 27:20, Leviticus 24:2 the lamp, 1 Kings 5:25 Solomon\'s); 28:5 names "the ephah" where Exodus 29:40 says "a tenth-part" (the delta computed); the parser reads 1/10 and 1/4. ONKELOS CONVERTS THE MEASURE: "one of ten in THREE SEAHS" — the ephah rendered as its three seahs (cut from the shelf\'s bytes; the translation\'s conversion layer, the walk\'s standing finding). The exam: Menachot 6:6-7 (the flours), Menachot 8:4-5 (the oils).')
srow(143, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"THE CONTINUAL BURNT OFFERING MADE AT MOUNT SINAI" — THE SINAI OLAH LIKENED TO THE TAMID: AS THE TAMID NEEDS LIBATIONS, SO DID SINAI\'S; R. YOSSI HAGELILI: LIKENED TO "A SWEET SAVOR", NOT TO THE LIBATIONS. 28:6 ties the daily offering to the one "made at Mount Sinai" (Exodus 24:5\'s burnt offerings at the covenant, or 29:38-42\'s command there — the row reads the covenant\'s): the LIKENING (hekkesh) runs one way — the Sinai olah takes the tamid\'s libations; R. Yossi HaGelili limits the likening to the "sweet savor" clause. The ink: "the [one] made" (העשיה) — the feminine participle\'s ONE seat in the Bible (computed); "at Mount Sinai" with the tamid here alone; the phrase "the continual burnt offering" (עלת התמיד) ELEVEN times in the two chapters, "a continual burnt offering" (עלת תמיד) at Exodus 29:42 and here (computed). The middah: the hekkesh (likening by juxtaposition in one verse — the E-codes\' comparison form), and its scope disputed. The exam: Chagigah 6a-b (the Sinai olah: "the olah that Israel offered in the wilderness — an olat tamid? R. Yishmael and R. Akiva"), Menachot 44b.')
srow(143, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"ITS LIBATION A QUARTER HIN FOR THE ONE LAMB" — POURED UNMIXED, NOT DILUTED; "IN THE HOLY PLACE POUR A LIBATION OF STRONG DRINK" — POURED ON THE ALTAR AND ABSORBED THERE; R. NATHAN: "POUR, A POURING" TWICE — TO INCLUDE THE WATER LIBATION. "Strong drink" (שכר) — undiluted wine (the Hebrew: "one you pour, not mixed"); "in the holy place... poured" — on the altar and swallowed in the altar (the Hebrew: "that they be poured in the holy and absorbed in the holy"); and R. NATHAN reads the doubled root "pour a pouring" (הסך נסך) as INCLUDING THE WATER LIBATION of Sukkot — the first of the three derivations of the water libation the piskaot carry (150:1 the other two). The ink: "a libation of strong drink" ONE seat; "pour" (הסך) one seat; "strong drink" eight Torah tokens — Leviticus 10:9 and Numbers 6:3\'s drink, 28:7\'s libation, and the WAGE-homograph at Genesis 30:16, Numbers 18:31, Deuteronomy 15:18, 23:5 (computed); ONKELOS: "a libation of OLD WINE" (cut). The middah: the doubled verb read for an addition (E10-class — the repetition as inclusion). The exam: Sukkah 34a, 44a, 48b-49b (the water libation, its two bowls, the "pour, pour"); Bava Batra 97a (the wine).')
srow(143, 3, 'MATERIAL', '"THE SECOND LAMB AT DUSK" — IF THE MORNING TAMID WAS NOT OFFERED, THE EVENING\'S IS NOT: UNLESS THE COURT ERRED OR WAS FORCED (R. SHIMON); THE INAUGURATIONS — THE GOLDEN ALTAR BY THE INCENSE OF DUSK, THE OUTER ALTAR BY THE MORNING TAMID, THE TABLE BY THE SABBATH\'S SHOWBREAD, THE LAMPSTAND BY ITS SEVEN LAMPS AT DUSK, AND VESSELS FINISHED EARLY WAIT FOR THEIR TIME; MOSES\' BLESSING AT THE WORK (EXODUS 39:43) WITH THE PSALM\'S WORDS; "THE SECOND" — SECOND TO THE TAMID, NOT TO THE PESACH: NOTHING BEFORE THE MORNING TAMID BUT THE INCENSE, NOTHING AFTER THE DUSK TAMID BUT THE INCENSE, THE PESACH AND THE ATONEMENT-LACKING ON PESACH EVE; "AS THE MEAL OFFERING OF THE MORNING" — THE LIBATIONS GO WITH EACH OFFERING; "A FIRE-OFFERING" — NOT ACCEPTED UNTIL WHOLLY BURNED; "A SWEET SAVOR" — I SPOKE AND MY WILL WAS DONE; "TO THE LORD" — BEN AZZAI: EVERY OFFERING NAMES THE UNIQUE NAME ALONE, NO OPENING FOR THE HERETICS; NO EATING BEFORE HIM. The longest row of the eleven (4,567 characters): (i) the dependence of the evening on the morning tamid — unwitting or forced courts excepted (R. Shimon); THE ENGLISH INSERTS "When is this so? When the altar had not been inaugurated..." — a clause the Hebrew row does not carry (the Mishnah Menachot 4:4\'s answer supplied by the translator; RESEARCH_LOG.md); (ii) THE INAUGURATIONS: each vessel by its first service — the incense altar by the dusk incense, the burnt-offering altar by the morning tamid, the TABLE by the Sabbath showbread (the Hebrew reads "and not that of THE FESTIVAL" — של חג ("of the festival") — where the sense is the table, השלחן ("the table"): a garbled word, RESEARCH_LOG.md), the lampstand by its seven lamps at dusk; R. Shimon: vessels finished before their time wait for their time — Exodus 39:43\'s "Moses saw all the work... and blessed them" with the blessing "may the Presence rest on the work of your hands" and Psalm 90:17 "and establish the work of our hands" — "not written in the Torah, explicit in the Writings" (the shelf\'s own data-channel label: a liturgy the ink does not carry, sourced to a psalm); (iii) "THE SECOND" — second to the tamid, not to the Pesach (Exodus 12:6\'s dusk): the order of the day — the incense before the morning tamid, and after the dusk tamid only the incense, the Pesach and the atonement-lacking on Pesach eve; (iv) "as the meal offering of the morning" (28:8 = Exodus 29:41, computed) — each offering\'s libation with it, not the two lambs first; (v) "a fire-offering" — no acceptance until burned; "a sweet savor" — the LORD\'s satisfaction "that I spoke and My will was done"; (vi) BEN AZZAI: every offering in the Torah names the Tetragrammaton alone — never Elohim, El, Shaddai, Tzevaot — "so as not to give the heretics an opening"; and "a sweet savor" said of the ox, the sheep and the bird alike — "no eating or drinking before Him" (Psalm 50:12-13, Leviticus 22:29). THE INK on ben Azzai\'s census: in the two chapters "to the LORD" (ליהוה) stands with every offering and Elohim never (computed: the divine name in 28-29 is the Tetragrammaton at every seat); "a pleasing aroma" eleven times, and ONKELOS renders every one "to be accepted with favor" (eleven of eleven, cut) — the translation\'s own "no eating before Him". The middot: the second-lamb argument (the dependence read from "the second"), the order-of-the-day rule, the inauguration rule, and ben Azzai\'s NAME CENSUS — a computed claim about the whole Torah\'s offerings, checkable (the compile\'s row). The exam: Menachot 4:4, 49a-50a (the temidim, the inaugurations), Pesachim 58a-59a (the order — nothing before the morning tamid, nothing after the evening), Menachot 110a (ben Azzai — the Unique Name on the offerings; "no eating"), Zevachim 46b.')
srow(144, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"ON THE SABBATH DAY TWO LAMBS" — THE SABBATH WAS INSIDE "THE ONE LAMB IN THE MORNING" AND IS TAKEN OUT OF ITS CLASS FOR A STRINGENCY, THE FIXED MUSAF; AND THE SERVICE OVERRIDES THE SABBATH: "MY SABBATHS YOU SHALL KEEP AND MY SANCTUARY YOU SHALL FEAR" (LEVITICUS 26:2) COULD MEAN EITHER — 28:9 DECIDES IT, FOR COMMUNAL OFFERINGS ONLY. The Sabbath\'s tamid was already commanded by 28:4; 28:9\'s two lambs are the MUSAF — an item taken out of its general rule to teach a stringency (the tenth of the thirteen middot: what left the class to be treated more stringently); and the Sabbath\'s own musaf proves that the Temple service overrides the Sabbath — Leviticus 26:2\'s two clauses could have run either way; "and on the Sabbath day two lambs" settles it for the COMMUNAL offering (the context — an individual\'s offering does not override). The ink: Leviticus 23:3\'s Sabbath has NO offering at all (rest and a holy convocation; computed) — 28:9-10 is the Sabbath\'s first offering in the Torah; "on the Sabbath day" (וביום השבת) with "two lambs" one seat. The middot: I10 (out of the class for a stringency) and the context rule ("in what does the passage speak? the communal"). The exam: Shabbat 132b, Yoma 85b, Pesachim 66a (the service overrides), Menachot 72b; Mishnah Temurah 2:1 (individual against communal).')
srow(144, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"THE BURNT OFFERING OF THE SABBATH ON ITS SABBATH" — NOT SABBATH EVE\'S ON THE SABBATH, NOT THIS SABBATH\'S ON ANOTHER: IF THE DAY PASSED, ITS OFFERING IS VOID; "BESIDES THE CONTINUAL BURNT OFFERING" — THE MUSAFIM BETWEEN THE TWO TEMIDIM. Three readings of the doubled noun: the Sabbath\'s limbs are not burned on the eve; a missed Sabbath is not made up on the next — "once the day has passed, its offering has passed"; and "besides the continual" places the musaf BETWEEN the morning and the evening tamid (the rule restated at 145:3 and 147:2 — the musafim only between). The ink: "the burnt offering of the Sabbath on its Sabbath" ONE seat, and "in its month" (28:14) one; the two possessive forms stand together elsewhere in ONE VERSE, Isaiah 66:23 ("from new moon to its new moon and from Sabbath to its Sabbath") — computed on the tokens. The middah: the "X on its X" restriction (E-class — the possessive read as a limit), the rule "if the day passed" a general principle drawn from it. The exam: Pesachim 58a (the musafim between), Menachot 49a; Berakhot 26a (the day that passed — the Sabbath\'s and the month\'s the exemplars of the "no make-up" rule).')
srow(145, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"AT THE BEGINNINGS OF YOUR MONTHS YOU SHALL OFFER A BURNT OFFERING" — THE NEW MOON TAKEN OUT OF THE TAMID\'S CLASS FOR ITS MUSAF, WITH ITS OWN NUMBER, NOT THE SABBATH\'S TWO; THE HIN IN THE TEMPLE WITH MARKS FOR THE BULL, THE RAM, THE LAMB (R. ELAZAR B. R. TZADOK) — OR NO HIN BUT A LOG-AND-A-HALF MEASURE FOR THE HIGH PRIEST\'S OFFERING (R. YISHMAEL). The new moon too was inside "the one lamb"; 28:11 takes it out for a stringency, and the argument "learn it from the Sabbath — two lambs" is refused: "Scripture speaks of ANOTHER NUMBER" (two bulls, a ram, seven lambs — the parser\'s [2, 1, 7]). Then THE HIN: R. Elazar b. R. Tzadok — a hin vessel stood in the Temple with graduation marks, "thus far for the bull (half), thus far for the ram (a third), thus far for the lamb (a quarter)" — 28:14\'s three fractions as marks on one vessel; R. Yishmael — no hin at all, but a log-and-a-half measure that divided the high priest\'s daily meal offering morning and evening. THE INK: the new moon is ABSENT from Leviticus 23 (no "month" token in the chapter as a feast; computed) — 28:11-15 is its whole register; "at the beginnings of your months" here and 10:10 (the trumpets — the two seats); the master table 3 / 2 / 1 tenths and 1/2 / 1/3 / 1/4 hin (28:12-14) restates Numbers 15:4-10\'s per-animal table (computed at both) and is what every later day cites "according to the ordinance". The middot: I10 (out of the class), the refused analogy (the "it would follow" turned back by the different number). The exam: Menachot 87b-88a (the hin and its marks; the log-and-a-half), Menachot 89a; Mishnah Menachot 9:2-3.')
srow(145, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"THIS IS THE BURNT OFFERING OF THE MONTH IN ITS MONTH" — NOT THIS MONTH\'S IN ANOTHER: THE DAY PASSED, THE OFFERING PASSED; "FOR THE MONTHS OF THE YEAR" — NOT THE MINIMUM OF "MONTHS", TWO, BUT EVERY MONTH. The Sabbath\'s rule (144:2) at the month: no make-up; and "for the months of the year" against the reading of the plural as two (the minimum of a plural — the tradition\'s counting rule: a plural means two unless enlarged). The ink: "the burnt offering of the month in its month" one seat (with Isaiah 66:23 the pair\'s other verse — computed); "for the months of the year" here and Exodus 12:2 ("the first of the months of the year" — the calendar\'s opening verse; computed): the two seats of the phrase are the month-count\'s definition and the month-offering\'s scope. ONKELOS: "the burnt offering of the head of the month AT ITS RENEWAL, so for all the heads of the months of the year" (cut) — "in its month" rendered "at its renewal". The middot: the possessive limit (E-class), and THE MINIMUM OF THE PLURAL IS TWO (a counting rule the shelf applies; MIDDOT.md). The exam: Menachot 49a, Berakhot 26a.')
srow(145, 3, 'MATERIAL', '"ONE GOAT FOR A SIN OFFERING TO THE LORD" — TO ATONE FOR THE GRAVE OF THE DEEP; "BESIDES THE CONTINUAL BURNT OFFERING" — THE MUSAFIM BETWEEN. The new moon\'s goat atones for "a grave of the deep" (קבר התהום) — a hidden impurity no one knew of when a holy thing was eaten in it (the Hebrew\'s term); the placement rule restated. THE INK: of the thirteen goats in the two chapters, the new moon\'s ALONE reads "a sin offering TO THE LORD" (28:15; computed on the six-token heads of the thirteen) — the festivals\' goats say "to atone for you" (28:22, 28:30, 29:5) or bare "a sin offering" (29:11-38): the exam\'s Shevuot 9a reads the words "to the LORD" as "a sin only the LORD knows" — the hidden impurity — and the Sifrei\'s "grave of the deep" is that reading\'s Sifrei form. The middah: the restrictor "to the LORD" read for the goat\'s object (E2-class). The exam: Shevuot 1:4-5, 9a-b (R. Shimon: "a sin offering to the LORD — the sin that only He knows"; the goats of the festivals and the new moon), Pesachim 80b-81a (the grave of the deep).')
srow(146, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"IN THE FIRST MONTH, ON THE FOURTEENTH DAY, PESACH TO THE LORD" — SCRIPTURE FIXES IT AS AN OBLIGATION; "SEVEN DAYS UNLEAVENED BREAD SHALL BE EATEN" — NOT ANY UNLEAVENED THING BUT WHAT CAN LEAVEN: THE FIVE GRAINS, NOT RICE, MILLET, SESAME. "Fixed as an obligation" (the Hebrew: קבעו הכתוב חובה — the row\'s two-word verdict on the date); "matzah shall be eaten" read with Deuteronomy 16:3 ("you shall not eat leaven with it; seven days you shall eat matzah with it") — only what can become leaven can be matzah: wheat, barley, spelt, oats, rye; rice, millet, sesame and poppy only rot. The ink: 28:16 = Leviticus 23:5 with "at dusk" DROPPED and "in the month" made "AND in the month" (six of eight tokens shared; computed); 28:17 = 23:6 with "the festival of unleavened bread to the LORD" shortened to "a festival" and "you shall eat" (plural) made "shall be eaten" (passive, singular — the form of Exodus 13:7 and Ezekiel 45:21; computed); "Pesach to the LORD" eleven Bible seats; the parser reads 14, then 15 and 7. The middah: the deduction from the paired command (leaven forbidden WITH the matzah — the two share a substance: E-class, the pairing). The exam: Pesachim 35a (the five grains), Mishnah Pesachim 2:5.')
srow(147, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"ON THE FIRST DAY A HOLY CONVOCATION" — HALLOW IT WITH FOOD, DRINK AND CLEAN CLOTHES; "NO LABORIOUS WORK" — WORK FORBIDDEN, AND FOOD-WORK PERMITTED BY THE IDENTITY "HOLY CONVOCATION" (EXODUS 12:16); "YOU SHALL OFFER A FIRE-OFFERING" — IF BULLS WERE FOUND AND NOT RAMS, OFFER WHAT IS FOUND: "EVEN ONE"; BUT WHEN ALL ARE FOUND, "TWO BULLS, ONE RAM". "Holy convocation" — the day\'s honor in eating, drinking and clean garments (the Sifra\'s form); "all laborious work you shall not do" forbids work — and the permission of food-preparation: the HEBREW derives it by IDENTITY OF EXPRESSION (I2) — "holy convocation" here, "holy convocation" at Exodus 12:16 where "only what every soul must eat may be done for you" stands — the ENGLISH cites Exodus 12:16 without the middah (RESEARCH_LOG.md); then THE AVAILABILITY RULE: bulls found but no rams, rams but no lambs — offer what there is, "even one of them" (the Hebrew\'s proof-text is LEVITICUS 23:8\'s "seven days you shall offer a fire-offering", the English rewrites it as 28:19 — recorded), and when all are found, the full number "two bulls, one ram" (28:19). THE INK: "laborious work" (מלאכת עבדה) at the six festival days of the two chapters (28:18, 25, 26; 29:1, 12, 35) and six of Leviticus 23 — against "any work" (כל מלאכה) at Yom Kippur (29:7), the Sabbath (Leviticus 23:3) and 23:31: the food-work delta is the ink\'s own two forms (computed); 28:18 = Leviticus 23:7 with "shall be to you" dropped (nine of nine shared). The middot: I2 (the identity "holy convocation"), and the "even one" ladder ("I might think... it is therefore written") at its FIRST of four seats in the span. The exam: Beitzah 12a, Chagigah 18a (the festival\'s work), Menachot 44b-45a (the offerings not indispensable to each other), Mishnah Menachot 4:4.')
srow(147, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"AS THESE YOU SHALL DO EACH DAY, SEVEN DAYS" — PESACH DOES NOT DECREASE AS SUKKOT DOES: THE ARGUMENT "BOTH NEED A FESTIVAL OFFERING; SUKKOT\'S DECREASES DAY BY DAY, SO SHOULD PESACH\'S" IS TURNED BACK — NEITHER ADD NOR SUBTRACT; THE MUSAFIM BETWEEN THE TEMIDIM. The "it would follow" (the qal va-chomer\'s cousin — the analogy from Sukkot\'s declining bulls, 29:13-32) refused by the verse: "as these" fixes every day of Pesach at the same table. THE INK: "as these you shall do each day" ONE seat (computed); Pesach\'s seven days share one verse (28:24) where Sukkot\'s seven take twenty-two (29:13-34) — the ink writes the constant table once and the declining table day by day; "bread, a fire-offering" (לחם אשה) here and Leviticus 3:11, 3:16 (the fat as "bread" — the tradition\'s "My bread" of 142:2). The middah: the refused analogy (a "it would follow" met by the verse — the form the Sifrei uses at 145:1 too). The exam: Menachot 45a; Sukkah 55b (the seventy bulls).')
srow(148, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"ON THE DAY OF THE FIRSTFRUITS, WHEN YOU OFFER A NEW MEAL OFFERING, IN YOUR WEEKS" — THE FIFTIETH DAY: COUNT FIFTY AND OFFER ON THE FIFTIETH, NOT THE FIFTY-FIRST; THE FESTIVAL ON THE SAME DAY — "IN YOUR WEEKS", NOT AFTER THEM; "A NEW MEAL OFFERING" — THE NEWEST OF ALL: NO MEAL OFFERING OF THE NEW CROP BEFORE IT — BEFORE THE OMER UNFIT, BEFORE THE TWO LOAVES FIT AFTER THE FACT; R. TARFON\'S ANALOGY AND YEHUDAH BEN NACHMAN\'S ANSWER; R. AKIVA: "YOUR FACE SHINES — I DOUBT YOU WILL LIVE LONG"; HE DIED BEFORE SHAVUOT. Leviticus 23:16\'s "count fifty days" is read to the day by 28:26: the offering ON the fiftieth (not the fifty-first), the festival the same day ("in your weeks" — within, not after); "a new meal offering" — the first of the new crop: the omer permits the crop to men, the two loaves permit it to the altar; a new-crop meal offering before the omer is unfit, before the two loaves fit after the fact; R. TARFON argues by analogy that both are unfit, YEHUDAH BEN NACHMAN answers with the difference (before the omer, forbidden to both; before the loaves, permitted to men), and R. Akiva\'s remark — the young man\'s shining face at besting the elder, "I doubt you will live long" — and R. Elazar b. R. Yehudah\'s report: by Shavuot he was gone. THE INK: "a new meal offering" TWO seats — Leviticus 23:16 and 28:26 (computed); "in your weeks" (בשבעתיכם) ONE seat; "the day of the firstfruits" the phrase\'s one seat as a date; ONKELOS RENDERS "IN YOUR WEEKS" AS "IN YOUR ASSEMBLIES" (בעצרתיכון — cut from the shelf\'s bytes): the translation gives Shavuot the tradition\'s own name ATZERET, the word the ink uses for Sukkot\'s eighth day (29:35) and Pesach\'s seventh (Deuteronomy 16:8). The middot: the second-formulation refusal ("not the second but the first"), the argument by analogy and its refutation (R. Tarfon / Yehudah b. Nachman — the dual-track carried). The exam: Menachot 65b-66a (the counting), Menachot 68b (the new meal offering before the omer and the two loaves — the Mishnah Menachot 10:6), Menachot 84b.')
srow(149, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"AND YOU SHALL OFFER A BURNT OFFERING FOR A SWEET SAVOR" — THESE BESIDES THOSE OF THE PRIESTS\' TORAH (LEVITICUS 23:18): NOT THE SAME OFFERINGS BUT ANOTHER SET; AND THE AVAILABILITY RULE AGAIN — "EVEN ONE", AND WHEN ALL ARE FOUND THE FULL NUMBER. Shavuot\'s musaf (28:27 — two bulls, a ram, seven lambs) is a SECOND set beside Leviticus 23:18\'s (seven lambs, a bull, two rams with the two loaves): "you cannot say the second formulation — are they alike?" — the two verses\' different numbers prove two sets (the English cites "Vayikra 27:18" — a slip for 23:18; the Hebrew "the Torah of the priests"); then "even one" (the Hebrew\'s proof-text again Leviticus 23:8; recorded). THE HEBREW ROW\'S OWN PROOF-TEXT IS GARBLED: it reads "bulls, sons of the herd, TWELVE, rams two" — Sukkot\'s SECOND DAY (29:17) — where 28:27 says "two bulls, one ram" (the English has it right): RESEARCH_LOG.md. THE INK: 28:27\'s table = 28:11\'s = 28:19\'s (2, 1, 7 — the parser reads the first and the third right, the second as [2, 8]: the gap named), and Leviticus 23:18\'s is (1, 2, 7) — the bulls and rams reversed between the two Shavuot tables (computed): two sets by the ink\'s own numbers. The middah: the two-verses-two-laws argument ("are they alike?"), and the "even one" ladder at its second seat. The exam: Menachot 45b (the two sets of Shavuot — "these besides those"), Mishnah Menachot 4:2.')
srow(149, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"UNBLEMISHED SHALL THEY BE FOR YOU, AND THEIR LIBATIONS" — THE LIBATIONS LIKENED TO THE ANIMAL: AS BLEMISH, DEFICIENCY OR EXCESS DISQUALIFIES THE BEAST, SO THE LIBATIONS. 28:31\'s syntax — "unblemished shall they be for you, and their libations" — joins the libations to the unblemished animals; the hekkesh: a libation short or over its measure is unfit as a blemished beast is. THE INK: "unblemished shall they be for you" three seats — 28:19, 28:31, 29:8 (computed); 28:31 alone adds "and their libations" after it. The middah: the hekkesh (likening in one clause). The exam: Menachot 87a-b (the measures of the libations — deficient or excessive), Mishnah Menachot 9:5.')
srow(150, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"ON THE FIFTEENTH DAY OF THE SEVENTH MONTH A HOLY CONVOCATION" — FIXED AS AN OBLIGATION; "EVEN ONE" A THIRD TIME (LEVITICUS 23:36 THE PROOF-TEXT); AND THE WATER LIBATION — THREE DERIVATIONS: R. AKIVA\'S INDUCTION (THE OMER FOR THE GRAIN, THE FIRSTFRUITS FOR THE TREES, SO WATER AT SUKKOT FOR THE RAINS), R. YEHUDAH BEN BETEIRA\'S THREE LETTERS (THE SECOND DAY\'S "AND THEIR LIBATIONS", THE SIXTH\'S "AND ITS LIBATIONS", THE SEVENTH\'S "ACCORDING TO THEIR ORDINANCE" — MEM, YOD, MEM: WATER), R. NATHAN\'S "POUR A POURING" (28:7). The date fixed; the availability rule at Sukkot\'s thirteen bulls; then the water libation\'s SOURCE disputed while the LAW stands — R. Akiva by the seasons\' pattern (an induction from two rites to a third — the E-class "as this so that" by pattern, the weakest form), R. YEHUDAH BEN BETEIRA (the English shortens him to "R. Yehudah" — RESEARCH_LOG.md) by THREE DEVIANT LETTERS in the ink, R. Nathan by the doubled root at 28:7. THE INK VERIFIES THE LETTERS EXACTLY: the seven days\' goat-verses close "its libation" (ונסכה) at days 1, 3, 4, 5, 7 (29:16, 22, 25, 28, 34) and the eighth day (29:38) — but the SECOND day reads "and THEIR libations" (ונסכיהם, 29:19 — an extra mem) and the SIXTH "and its libations" (ונסכיה, 29:31 — an extra yod; the form\'s ONE seat in the Bible); the ordinance-verses read "according to the ordinance" (כמשפט) at days 2 through 6 and the eighth (29:18, 21, 24, 27, 30, 37) — but the SEVENTH day reads "according to THEIR ordinance" (כמשפטם, 29:33 — an extra mem): mem, yod, mem — מים, "water" — computed on the tokens of all fifteen verses. The middot: R. Akiva\'s induction (the pattern argument), the LETTER READ (the deviant spelling as data — M-class: the ink\'s variation carrying a law; the notarikon (the reading of scattered letters as a word) of three letters across three verses), R. Nathan\'s doubled verb (E10-class). The exam: Taanit 2b-3a (the three letters, R. Yehudah b. Beteira; R. Akiva\'s water), Shabbat 103b (the letters as a Torah source), Sukkah 34a, 44a, 48b-51a (the water libation and its joy), Zevachim 110b; Rosh Hashanah 16a (the water for the rains).')
srow(151, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"ON THE EIGHTH DAY AN ASSEMBLY (ATZERET) SHALL BE FOR YOU" — SCRIPTURE WITHHELD HIM FROM LEAVING: ONE WHO BROUGHT HIS OFFERINGS FROM BETH-PAGGI TO JERUSALEM EATS THEM THERE AND SLEEPS THERE, NOT OUTSIDE; "WITHHOLDING" IS CONFINEMENT (JEREMIAH 36:5, 33:1); AND THE FIRST DAY THE SAME, BY THE SHARED "HOLY CONVOCATION". The word עצרת read from its root — "withheld" (עצר): the pilgrim is held in Jerusalem overnight (Deuteronomy 16:7\'s "in the morning you shall turn and go to your tents" the English\'s gloss); Jeremiah\'s two "confined" (עצור) the semantic proof; and the first festival day gets the same by the shared name "holy convocation" (an "it follows" from the common term). THE INK: "assembly" (עצרת) three Torah seats — Leviticus 23:36 and 29:35 for Sukkot\'s eighth day, Deuteronomy 16:8 for PESACH\'S SEVENTH (computed); ONKELOS renders it "a GATHERING" (כנישו — cut) — and gave Shavuot the same name at 28:26; 29:35 = Leviticus 23:36 nine of ten shared (computed). The middot: the word\'s meaning fixed by its use elsewhere (the lexical proof from the Prophets), and the analogy from the shared term (the "it follows" on "holy convocation"). The exam: Sukkah 47a-48a (the eighth day a festival of its own — "the lot, the blessing, the festival, the offering, the song, the time"; the overnight stay — Pesachim 95b, Rosh Hashanah 5a), Chagigah 17a (atzeret).')
srow(151, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"AND YOU SHALL OFFER A BURNT OFFERING" — THE AVAILABILITY RULE A FOURTH TIME: BULLS AND NO RAMS, RAMS AND NO LAMBS — "EVEN ONE" (LEVITICUS 23:8 THE HEBREW\'S PROOF-TEXT AGAIN); WHEN ALL ARE FOUND, "ONE BULL, ONE RAM". The same ladder at the eighth day\'s table (29:36 — one bull, one ram, seven lambs; the parser [1, 1, 7]): the FOURTH seat of the "even one" argument in the eleven piskaot (147:1, 149:1, 150:1, 151:2) — one rule stated at every table, the Hebrew citing LEVITICUS 23:8\'s "seven days you shall offer" each time (the English varies the citation; RESEARCH_LOG.md). THE INK: the eighth day\'s table is the new moon\'s (28:11) without the second bull (computed: [1, 1, 7] against [2, 1, 7]) — Rosh Hashanah\'s and Yom Kippur\'s the same three (29:2, 29:8). The middah: the "I might think... it is therefore written" ladder (the E-codes\' form), and the CROSS-BOOK teacher: the Sifrei on Numbers proving Numbers\' tables from Leviticus 23\'s clause — a TRANSFER with a teacher, the compile\'s edge 28-29 → Leviticus 23 (the link review law\'s REFERENCE class). The exam: Menachot 44b-45a, Mishnah Menachot 4:4.')
srow(152, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"THESE YOU SHALL DO TO THE LORD IN YOUR APPOINTED TIMES, BESIDES YOUR VOWS" — THE VOWS AND FREEWILL OFFERINGS VOWED ON THE FESTIVAL ARE BROUGHT ON THE FESTIVAL (THE YEAR\'S VOWS DEUTERONOMY 12:5-6 ALREADY COVERS); "BESIDES YOUR VOWS" — THE BIRD-PAIRS OF THE ZAVIM AND ZAVOT, PERMITTED ON THE FESTIVAL; "AND MOSES SAID TO THE CHILDREN OF ISRAEL" (30:1) — TO CLOSE THE MATTER: WITHOUT IT, "BESIDES YOUR VOWS... AND MOSES SPOKE" (30:2) WOULD NOT SAY WHERE THE VOWS\' CHAPTER BEGINS — R. YISHMAEL. 29:39\'s "these" are the festival\'s own vows — vowed on it, brought on it — since Deuteronomy 12 covers the year\'s; "besides your vows" includes the zav\'s and zavah\'s bird-pairs (Leviticus 15) brought on a festival; and 30:1 is a PARAGRAPH CLOSER: R. Yishmael reads the frame "and Moses said to the children of Israel according to all that the LORD commanded Moses" as PUNCTUATION — it closes the calendar so that 30:2\'s "and Moses spoke to the heads of the tribes" opens the vows and is not read with 29:39\'s "vows". THE INK: 29:39 = Leviticus 23:37-38\'s close rewritten (two of eleven tokens shared with 23:37; 23:38\'s "besides the Sabbaths of the LORD and besides your gifts and besides all your vows and besides all your freewill offerings" here "besides your vows and your freewill offerings, your burnt offerings, your meal offerings, your libations and your peace offerings" — the four offering-kinds listed; computed); "besides your vows" (לבד מנדריכם) one seat; 30:1 read with the next draft (the draft\'s-grain rule) — its clause "according to all that the LORD commanded Moses" the RECEIPT formula the register gate counts (the gate\'s receipt class at 30:1, not one of the 58 "as the LORD commanded" lines — the form differs: "according to ALL that"); ONKELOS: "the sacrifices of your holy things" for "your peace offerings" (cut). The middot: the scope decided by the other verse (Deuteronomy 12 taking the year\'s vows, leaving the festival\'s to this), and THE FRAME AS A CLOSER — a structural rule about the ink\'s own sentence boundaries (R. Yishmael), the ink\'s "these are..." footer form (the register gate\'s headers: 29:39 "these you shall do" is a CLOSE of the calendar — the ARCHITECTURE measurement\'s footer class). The exam: Beitzah 19a-20b (vows and freewill offerings on a festival — Beit Shammai and Beit Hillel), Mishnah Beitzah 2:4, Rosh Hashanah 4b-5a (the festival\'s vows and "you shall not delay"), Pesachim 59a (the bird-pairs).')
